## 1. What Are Static Pods?

 A Static Pod is a Pod managed directly by the Kubelet on a node — not by the API server or control plane.

 Control plane components are usually Static Pods
### Why Kubernetes Uses Static Pods
 Because API Server itself may not be available initially.

 So kubelet directly starts:

 API server
 etcd
 scheduler

 without depending on Kubernetes API. This is called: Self-hosted bootstrap


 They are usually used for:

 Bootstrapping control plane components (like kube-apiserver, etcd, etc.)

 Running critical node-level services that must always exist

 The Kubelet watches a directory for static pod manifests: /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

 Any YAML file you put here → Kubelet automatically creates that Pod.
### Mirror Pod Concept
 When a static pod runs, Kubelet creates a “mirror pod” entry in the API server so you can see it with kubectl. API server gets a read-only copy
 But that mirror pod cannot be edited or deleted — it’s read-only.


 Manifest  File in worker node
       ↓
 /etc/kubernetes/manifests
       ↓
 Kubelet Watches Folder
       ↓
 Creates Static Pod
       ↓
 Mirror Pod appears in API Server
### Static Pod Flow
 1️⃣ You drop a YAML file in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/
 2️⃣ Kubelet detects the file → creates the Pod directly
 3️⃣ Kubelet requests a “mirror pod” in the API server for visibility
 4️⃣ If you delete the mirror pod → static pod keeps running
### Static Pods vs Normal Pods

 | Feature        | Static Pod                                       | Normal Pod                       |
 | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------- |
 | Managed by     | **Kubelet** directly                             | **Kube-API Server**              |
 | Created via    | YAML file on node (`/etc/kubernetes/manifests/`) | `kubectl create`                  |
 | Scheduler      | Not scheduled — runs **on that node only**       | Scheduled by **kube-scheduler**  |
 | Replication    | No ReplicaSet or Deployment                      | Managed by ReplicaSet/Deployment |
 | API Visibility | Shows in `kubectl get pods` (via mirror pod)     | Fully managed by API             |
 | Use case       | System components, node-local apps               | User workloads                   |

