## 3. Kubernetes Health Probes
 Health Probes are Kubernetes mechanisms that allow the kubelet to determine whether a container/pod is healthy, ready to receive traffic, or has started successfully.


 Health Probes are used by Kubernetes to check: Is application healthy and ready?

 Sometimes container is running BUT application inside failed

 Without probes: ❌ Kubernetes thinks pod is healthy


 Health probes are how Kubernetes checks if your containers are healthy and ready to serve traffic.
 They help Kubernetes decide:

 🚦 When to start sending traffic to a container

 🧹 When to restart a container that’s unhealthy

 3 Types

 | Probe               | Purpose                                    | What Kubernetes does if it fails                         |
 | ------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------- |
 | **Startup Probe**   | Has the application finished starting?     | Delays liveness/readiness checks until startup completes |
 | **Readiness Probe** | Is the application ready to serve traffic? | Removes the Pod from Service endpoints                   |
 | **Liveness Probe**  | Is the application still alive?            | Restarts the container                                   |






 | Probe           | Purpose
 | --------------- | -----------------------------
 | Startup Probe   | Has app started successfully? used for slow starting applications
 | Readiness Probe | Ready to accept traffic?  / Can app receive traffic?, if probe fails, Pod NOT restarted instead remove from service
                                                 endpoint. No traffic sent, it will stop traffic
 | Liveness Probe  | Is app alive? / Is application still alive? if probe fails, k8s restarts container
### Types of Health Probes

 livenessProbe:
 Checks if the app is alive. If it fails → pod is restarted.

 Example: App is hung or deadlocked or application gets stuck in an infinite loop. A liveness probe detects this.

 Probe fails
    ↓
 kubelet detects
    ↓
 Container restarted


 readinessProbe:
 ---------------.
 A readiness probe determines whether a Pod should receive traffic.

 Checks If the POD is ready to serve requests. If it fails → pod removed from service endpoints.
 Example:  App still starting up or warming cache.

 Probe fails
    ↓
 Pod removed from endpoints
    ↓
 No traffic routed


 startupProbe:
 Some applications take a long time to start.

 Used For slow-starting apps. Checks if the app has *started successfully* before liveness/readiness begin.
 Example: Large Java app booting slowly.

 Some applications take a long time to start.

 For example: MySQL , Elasticsearch, Kafka

 Suppose MySQL needs 60 seconds to initialize.

 Without a startup probe:

 0 sec   Container starts

 10 sec  Liveness Probe
          FAIL

 20 sec  FAIL

 30 sec  FAIL

 Container restarted

 It never gets enough time to finish starting.

 A startup probe solves this.

 Container starts
       |
 Startup Probe
       |
       X
       X
       X
       ✓
       |
 Enable Liveness Probe

 Only after the startup probe succeeds do the liveness and readiness probes begin.
### How Kubernetes Performs Probes
 You can define probes in 3 ways:

 HTTP Probe: Calls a specific HTTP endpoint and checks for a 200–399 status code. Example: /health, /status
 TCP Probe: Checks if a TCP socket is open on a port. Example: Useful for non-HTTP apps.
 Exec Probe: Runs a command inside the container; success if exit code = 0. Example: e.g., pgrep nginx

 | Parameter             | Meaning                                                                      |
 | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
 | `initialDelaySeconds` | Wait before the first probe after the container starts                       |
 | `periodSeconds`       | How often to run the probe                                                   |
 | `timeoutSeconds`      | How long to wait for a response before considering the probe failed          |
 | `failureThreshold`    | Number of consecutive failures before taking action                          |
 | `successThreshold`    | Number of consecutive successes required (mainly used with readiness probes) |


 Startup Probe: Gives slow-starting applications enough time to initialize.
 Liveness Probe: Restarts the container if it becomes unhealthy or hangs.
 Readiness Probe: Ensures only healthy, ready Pods receive traffic from Services.


 Visual Flow
        ┌─────────────────────────────┐
        │ Container starts            │       |
        └────────────┬────────────────┘
                         |
                  startupProbe runs
                          │
      ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
      │ Success                     │ Fail
