### Example: All Three Probes in One Pod

```yaml
 apiVersion: v1
 kind: Pod
 metadata:
   name: probe-demo
 spec:
   containers:
   - name: web
     image: nginx
     ports:
     - containerPort: 80
```

     #1️⃣ Startup Probe (wait for app to boot)
     startupProbe:
       httpGet:
         path: /
         port: 80
       failureThreshold: 30  # Try for ~5 min
       periodSeconds: 10

     # 2️⃣ Liveness Probe (restart if dead)
     livenessProbe:
       httpGet:
         path: /
         port: 80
       initialDelaySeconds: 30
       periodSeconds: 10
       failureThreshold: 3

     # 3️⃣ Readiness Probe (only send traffic when ready)
     readinessProbe:
       httpGet:
         path: /
         port: 80
       initialDelaySeconds: 10
       periodSeconds: 5
       failureThreshold: 3



 initialDelaySeconds 30: Kubernetes waits 30 seconds after the container starts before checking health. Time Kubernetes waits before first probe
 periodSeconds 10 : Kubernetes checks the health every 10 seconds.How often probe runs.
 failureThreshold 3 : If the probe fails 3 times in a row, Kubernetes takes action.
### Demo: Liveness Probe Failure and Auto-Restart

 We’ll create a simple container that:

 Starts an HTTP server

 Then “breaks” after 30 seconds

 Kubernetes will detect this via livenessProbe and restart it 💡

 vi liveness-demo.yml

```yaml
 apiVersion: v1
 kind: Pod
 metadata:
   name: liveness-demo
 spec:
   containers:
   - name: liveness-demo
     image: busybox
     args:
       - /bin/sh
       - -c
       - |
         echo "Starting server...";
         # Create dummy health endpoint
         (while true; do
           echo -e "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\n\nOK" | nc -l -p 8080 -q 1;
         done) &
         sleep 30; # after 30 seconds, stop the server
         echo "Simulating crash..."
         pkill nc;
         sleep 3600;
```

     livenessProbe:
       httpGet:
         path: /
         port: 8080
       initialDelaySeconds: 5
       periodSeconds: 5
       failureThreshold: 2

 Script Explanation:
 Starts a background process (notice the & at the end).
 The while true loop runs forever — unless stopped.
 Uses nc (netcat) to open a simple TCP server on port 8080.
 When a client connects, it sends  HTTP/1.1 200 OK
 and closes the connection (-q 1 means quit 1 second after EOF)
 pkill nc: This kills the netcat (nc) process, which means your HTTP server stops running.

 kubectl apply -f liveness-demo.yml

 kubectl get pods

 After ~50 seconds:

 kubectl get pods

 Kubernetes detected the container crash and restarted it automatically.

 kubectl describe pod liveness-demo

 kubectl delete pod liveness-demo
